  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nPrecipitation has ended across the Quad State at this time. Low\nclouds remain in southern portions of Western Kentucky. Despite\nthe heavy rainfall Friday, sufficient surface winds during the\nnight should keep visibility decreases minimal. Today, high\npressure moves through the Great Lakes area, keeping\nnortheasterly flow across the Quad State and limiting highs to\nthe mid-70s. While clouds move in from the southwest, the front\nand better precipitation potential will be across TN or further\nsouth, resulting in a dry forecast for the entire Quad State. On\nSunday, a second cold front reinforces dry cooler air as a \nbroad area of high pressure moves towards the Midwest from the \nNorthern Plains. \n\nDry weather and high pressure is favored for the entire workweek\nahead. The coolest mornings will be Tuesday and Wednesday with\nmorning lows in the 40s. A ridge axis moves through Tuesday \nwhich will allow for a bit more of a southerly flow afterwards,\nand a slow warming trend as a subsequent ridge builds over the\nQuad State late week. Models point towards late next weekend as\nthe next chance for rain.",
